[119.55022, "o", "rting chronology\r\n   223: \r\n   224: Regional lead E. Novak hosted a readout on capital project capitalization where finance controllers disputed whether unsigned meeting notes should override ledger owners; investigators cited Brief 05 during the session.\r\n   225: \r\n   226: Site visit #525 to fixed assets exported 19 compliance threads tied to capital project capitalization. Reviewers noted that owner field churn appeared whenever correction batches straddled a weekend wire cutoff.\r\n   227: \r\n   228: Grant compliance WP-9559 tied capital project capitalization attestation gaps to ledger slice confusion visible only when hold flags used corrected rather than pre-correction ledger dates.\r\n   229: \r\n   230: Peer review #804 of fixed assets sampling found 19 mislinked emails on capital project capitalization. None of the mislinked messages originated from compliance addresses, supporting Brief 02 sender restrictions.\r\n   231: \r\n\r\n--- context around line 220 ---\r\n   214: ## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Preced"]
[119.550547, "o", "ence\r\n   215: \r\n   216: Interview notes with the compliance desk confirmed status fights between mail,\r\n   217: amendments, and corrections. Ledger rows provide the base status (lowercase on\r\n   218: output). Status precedence from lowest to highest is: pending, approved,\r\n   219: rejected, reversed. Email excerpts may change status only when the From line\r\n   220: contains `compliance@` anywhere in the address; ignore all other senders.\r\n   221: \r\n   222: ## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence — supporting chronology\r\n   223: \r\n   224: Regional lead E. Novak hosted a readout on capital project capitalization where finance controllers disputed whether unsigned meeting notes should override ledger owners; investigators cited Brief 05 during the session.\r\n   225: \r\n   226: Site visit #525 to fixed assets exported 19 compliance threads tied to capital project capitalization. Reviewers noted that owner field churn appeared whenever correction batches straddled a weekend wire cutoff.\r\n   227: \r\n   228: "]
[119.550861, "o", "Grant compliance WP-9559 tied capital project capitalization attestation gaps to ledger slice confusion visible only when hold flags used corrected rather than pre-correction ledger dates.\r\n   229: \r\n   230: Peer review #804 of fixed assets sampling found 19 mislinked emails on capital project capitalization. None of the mislinked messages originated from compliance addresses, supporting Brief 02 sender restrictions.\r\n   231: \r\n   232: Committee packet #320 chronicles how capital project capitalization escalated after R. Okonkwo observed retroactive status conflict between two correction batches. Cross-checks against amendment minutes were required before accepting any owner change.\r\n   233: \r\n   234: External advisors reviewing capital project capitalization during July review asked for a machine-readable trace explaining why rejected rows received hold flags. The archive preserves narrative chronology even though only structured tail sections are authoritative.\r\n   235: \r\n   236: SOX testing team WP-4584 li"]
[119.551107, "o", "nked mailbox ingestion lag on capital project capitalization to a manual override logged at 02:14 local time between two automated correction imports.\r\n   237: \r\n   238: Data governance WP-7874 catalogs legacy fixed assets folders still containing capital project capitalization spreadsheets. Those folders are evidentiary only; extractor output must come from canonical structured sections.\r\n   239: \r\n   240: Memo WP-5125 summarizes a panel on capital project capitalization chaired by A. Ndiaye. Participants debated whether informal spreadsheets should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 closes that debate for the FY24 extractor.\r\n\r\n--- context around line 977 ---\r\n   971: Grant compliance WP-3449 tied inventory obsolescence reserve attestation gaps to effective-date mismatch visible only when hold flags used corrected rather than pre-correction ledger dates.\r\n   972: \r\n   973: ## Investigation Brief 07 — Exception Flags (Part I)\r\n   974: \r\n   975: Exception coding review: `exception_reason` is null unless "]
[119.551345, "o", "a code applies. Set\r\n   976: `over_limit` when `amount_usd` strictly exceeds 10000. Set `compliance_hold`\r\n   977: when final status is `rejected`, a qualifying `compliance@` email referenced\r\n   978: that transaction, and that email block includes a `sent:` line. The sent-date\r\n   979: comparison used for `compliance_hold` is defined in Investigation Brief 09\r\n   980: (Mid-Year Amendment), not Brief 03. Multiple reasons join with semicolon in\r\n   981: lexical order of the reason codes.\r\n   982: \r\n   983: ## Investigation Brief 07 — Exception Flags (Part I) — supporting chronology\r\n   984: \r\n   985: Stakeholder workshop #199 on revenue cutoff testing produced conflicting recollections about which correction notice superseded an earlier owner change; Brief 04 governs that precedence for automated output.\r\n   986: \r\n   987: During September fieldwork, R. Okonkwo compared two cold extractor runs and documented correction batch ordering on revenue cutoff testing. Operations initially attributed the drift to c"]
[119.551367, "o", "ache state; QA disproved that hypothesis the following morning.\r\n   988: \r\n   989: Fixed-assets specialist L. Bergstrom argued that revenue cutoff testing capitalization memos should not alter ledger status; investigators agreed while noting those memos still inform exception coding narratives.\r\n   990: \r\n   991: Lead reviewer P. Okafor opened working paper WP-6447 after external audit reported that revenue cutoff testing distorted the September fieldwork reconciliation. The team reconstructed mailbox ordering and found correction batch ordering affecting at least 15 transaction threads.\r\n   992: \r\n   993: Follow-up #859 confirmed that external audit routed revenue cutoff testing statements through a shared inbox with 16 delegates. Investigators flagged policy waiver omission as the likely root cause of inconsistent status columns.\r\n   994: \r\n   995: Quality review #106 sampled 24 revenue cutoff testing tickets and found ledger slice confusion whenever provisional ledger rows were not filtered before merge.\r\n"]
