[122.567155, "o", "\r\n=== policy context near line 3 ===\r\n     1: # FY24 Internal Audit Archive\r\n     2: \r\n     3: Mixed investigation briefs, committee minutes, mail excerpts, policy exceptions, correction notices, and ledger rows. Extractor policy is distributed across the file.\r\n     4: \r\n     5: ## Reconciliation and Exception Policy (FY24 Audit Handbook)\r\n     6: \r\n     7: This index orients readers only. Reconciliation rules appear inside Investigation Briefs 01–08 and the Mid-Year Amendment in Brief 09. Draft narrative, decoy sections, and archived threads are not authoritative. Structured source data appears near the file end under Email Excerpts, Policy Exceptions, Correction Notices, and the canonical Transaction Ledger heading.\r\n     8: \r\n     9: Brief 09 supersedes earlier hold-timing wording in narrative drafts and partially defers Brief 07 on compliance_hold date comparison.\r\n"]
[122.567173, "o", "    10: \r\n"]
[122.567241, "o", "    11: ## Investigation Brief 01 — Ledger Sourcing\r\n    12: \r\n"]
[122.567293, "o", "    13: Finance QA traced rerun drift to parsers that latched onto the wrong ledger\r\n    14: slice. For extractor output, treat only the markdown section whose heading\r\n"]
[122.567588, "o", "    15: line is exactly `## Transaction Ledger` as authoritative. The archive also\r\n"]
[122.56762, "o", "    16: contains a working-copy ledger and prose that mentions ledger headings; those\r\n    17: are investigative context only.\r\n    18: \r\n    19: Ignore any ledger block that includes a line `provisional: true` (case\r\n    20: insensitive). When the same transaction id appears in multiple non-provisional\r\n    21: ledger blocks, the last such block in file order is the base row for that id.\r\n    22: \r\n    23: Locate section boundaries with regular expressions anchored to whole heading\r\n    24: lines at line start. Do not locate sections with indexOf or substring search.\r\n    25: \r\n    26: ## Investigation Brief 01 — Ledger Sourcing — supporting chronology\r\n    27: \r\n    28: Audit technologist T. Singh replayed FY24 mailbox snapshots and showed how inventory obsolescence reserve threads arrived out of order relative to correction notices, surfacing effective-date mismatch on four high-balance rows.\r\n    29: \r\n    30: Controller staff described inventory obsolescence reserve as a secondary driver of mailbox i"]
[122.567639, "o", "ngestion lag while rebuilding the October count close calendar. They emphasized that decoy ledger headings in draft appendices must be ignored.\r\n    31: \r\n    32: Treasury liaison E. Novak explained that inventory obsolescence reserve related wires were paused during October count, delaying compliance responses and amplifying mailbox ingestion lag on rejected rows.\r\n"]
[122.567662, "o", "    33: \r\n"]
[122.567674, "o", "    34: Internal audit follow-up #145 tracked how inventory obsolescence reserve exceptions were closed without matching policy waiver paperwork, a separate issue from extractor merge ordering.\r\n"]
[122.567694, "o", "    35: \r\n"]
[122.567715, "o", "    36: Stakeholder workshop #818 on inventory obsolescence reserve produced conflicting recollections about which correction notice superseded an earlier owner change; Brief 04 governs that precedence for automated output.\r\n"]
[122.568504, "o", "    37: \r\n    38: Platform engineer J. Huang noted that inventory obsolescence reserve webhook retries duplicated compliance messages in the investigative export, requiring deduplication by subject and sent timestamp during human review.\r\n    39: \r\n    40: Data governance WP-3763 catalogs legacy operations finance folders still containing inventory obsolescence reserve spreadsheets. Those folders are evidentiary only; extractor output must come from canonical structured sections.\r\n    41: \r\n    42: SOX testing team WP-6621 linked policy waiver omission on inventory obsolescence reserve to a manual override logged at 02:14 local time between two automated correction imports.\r\n    43: \r\n    44: Peer review #417 of operations finance sampling found 14 mislinked emails on inventory obsolescence reserve. None of the mislinked messages originated from compliance addresses, supporting Brief 02 sender restrictions.\r\n    45: \r\n    46: Tax counsel flagged inventory obsolescence reserve restatement risk during October c"]
[122.568519, "o", "ount close, unrelated to extractor precedence but relevant to why the archive retains full correspondence.\r\n    47: \r\n    48: Interview #743 with operations finance counsel captured how inventory obsolescence reserve correspondence referenced amounts near $45,275 without matching ledger rows. Investigators preserved the thread because hold flag suppression can change downstream exception coding.\r\n    49: \r\n    50: Vendor management L. Bergstrom described inventory obsolescence reserve onboarding delays that pushed compliance responses past ledger dates on several rejected rows during October count.\r\n    51: \r\n    52: Follow-up #384 confirmed that operations finance routed inventory obsolescence reserve statements through a shared inbox with 5 delegates. Investigators flagged ledger slice confusion as the likely root cause of inconsistent status columns.\r\n    53: \r\n\r\n=== policy context near line 7 ===\r\n     1: # FY24 Internal Audit Archive\r\n     2: \r\n     3: Mixed investigation briefs, committee minutes, mail "]
[122.56853, "o", "excerpts, policy exceptions, correction notices, and ledger rows. Extractor policy is distributed across the file.\r\n     4: \r\n     5: ## Reconciliation and Exception Policy (FY24 Audit Handbook)\r\n     6: \r\n     7: This index orients readers only. Reconciliation rules appear inside Investigation Briefs 01–08 and the Mid-Year Amendment in Brief 09. Draft narrative, decoy sections, and archived threads are not authoritative. Structured source data appears near the file end under Email Excerpts, Policy Exceptions, Correction Notices, and the canonical Transaction Ledger heading.\r\n     8: \r\n     9: Brief 09 supersedes earlier hold-timing wording in narrative drafts and partially defers Brief 07 on compliance_hold date comparison.\r\n    10: \r\n    11: ## Investigation Brief 01 — Ledger Sourcing\r\n    12: \r\n    13: Finance QA traced rerun drift to parsers that latched onto the wrong ledger\r\n    14: slice. For extractor output, treat only the markdown section whose heading\r\n    15: line is exactly `## Transaction Le"]
[122.56854, "o", "dger` as authoritative. The archive also\r\n    16: contains a working-copy ledger and prose that mentions ledger headings; those\r\n    17: are investigative context only.\r\n    18: \r\n    19: Ignore any ledger block that includes a line `provisional: true` (case\r\n    20: insensitive). When the same transaction id appears in multiple non-provisional\r\n    21: ledger blocks, the last such block in file order is the base row for that id.\r\n    22: \r\n    23: Locate section boundaries with regular expressions anchored to whole heading\r\n    24: lines at line start. Do not locate sections with indexOf or substring search.\r\n"]
