[542.746854, "o", "1:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence — supporting chronology\r\n7:Draft sidebar (non-authoritative): some prototype tooling compared compliance mail timestamps against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. Brief 09 later in this archive supersedes that draft practice for production reconciliation.\r\n9:Treasury liaison J. Huang explained that payroll accrual true-up related wires were paused during May journal, delaying compliance responses and amplifying status precedence inversion on rejected rows.\r\n11:Stakeholder workshop #718 on payroll accrual true-up produced conflicting recollections about which correction notice superseded an earlier owner change; Brief 04 governs that precedence for automated output.\r\n23:Grant compliance WP-9154 tied payroll accrual true-up attestation gaps to mailbox ingestion lag visible only when hold flags used corrected rather than pre-correction ledger dates.\r\n29:During May journal, J. Huang compared two cold extractor runs and documented st"]
[542.75012, "o", "atus precedence inversion on payroll accrual true-up. Operations initially attributed the drift to cache state; QA disproved that hypothesis the following morning.\r\n37:Operations analyst P. Okafor demonstrated that payroll accrual true-up batches processed after midnight UTC inherited stale owner fields, a symptom consistent with status precedence inversion rather than incorrect amount parsing.\r\n47:External advisors reviewing payroll accrual true-up during May journal asked for a machine-readable trace explaining why rejected rows received hold flags. The archive preserves narrative chronology even though only structured tail sections are authoritative.\r\n53:Tax counsel flagged payroll accrual true-up restatement risk during May journal close, unrelated to extractor precedence but relevant to why the archive retains full correspondence.\r\n65:External advisors reviewing capital project capitalization during July review asked for a machine-readable trace explaining why rejected rows received hold flags. The archi"]
[542.750161, "o", "ve preserves narrative chronology even though only structured tail sections are authoritative.\r\n73:During July review, P. Okafor compared two cold extractor runs and documented status precedence inversion on capital project capitalization. Operations initially attributed the drift to cache state; QA disproved that hypothesis the following morning.\r\n75:Grant compliance WP-4261 tied capital project capitalization attestation gaps to owner field churn visible only when hold flags used corrected rather than pre-correction ledger dates.\r\n81:Stakeholder workshop #276 on capital project capitalization produced conflicting recollections about which correction notice superseded an earlier owner change; Brief 04 governs that precedence for automated output.\r\n83:Tax counsel flagged capital project capitalization restatement risk during July review close, unrelated to extractor precedence but relevant to why the archive retains full correspondence.\r\n89:Lead reviewer K. Morales opened working paper WP-5655 after fixed ass"]
[542.750174, "o", "ets reported that capital project capitalization distorted the July review reconciliation. The team reconstructed mailbox ordering and found status precedence inversion affecting at least 11 transaction threads.\r\n"]
